## 1.  What is Entity Framework Core?

Entity Framework Core (EF Core) is Microsoft’s “Object‑Relational Mapper” (ORM) for .NET.  
It lets you work **with a relational database as if it were a collection of C# objects**:

* **Define your domain model** – POCO classes that represent tables (e.g., `Blog`, `Post`).
* **Create a `DbContext`** – a class that knows how to map those classes to database tables.
* **Write LINQ queries** – strongly‑typed, compile‑time checked, and translated into SQL on the fly.
* **Persist changes** – call `SaveChanges()` and EF Core generates the necessary `INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE` statements.

EF Core supports many database providers (SQL Server, PostgreSQL, SQLite, MySQL, etc.) and
works in all kinds of .NET applications – console apps, ASP.NET Core, Blazor, MAUI, etc.

Below we’ll walk through a **complete, minimal example** that uses SQLite (file‑based) so that you can
run it on any machine without setting up a separate DB server.

---

## 2.  Project Setup

### 2.1  Create a new console app

```bash
dotnet new console -n EFCoreDemo
cd EFCoreDemo
```

### 2.2  Add EF Core packages

```bash
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite
dotnet add package Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design
```

* **`Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Sqlite`** – provider for SQLite.  
  Replace it with `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer` if you want SQL Server.
* **`Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Design`** – tools needed for migrations (`dotnet ef`).

---

## 3.  Domain Model – Two Entities

```csharp
// Models/Blog.cs
namespace EFCoreDemo.Models
{
    public class Blog
    {
        public int BlogId { get; set; }            // PK (convention: <ClassName>Id)
        public string Url { get; set; } = null!;   // non‑nullable string

        // Navigation property – one blog has many posts
        public ICollection<Post> Posts { get; } = new List<Post>();
    }
}
```

```csharp
// Models/Post.cs
namespace EFCoreDemo.Models
{
    public class Post
    {
        public int PostId { get; set; }            // PK
        public string Title { get; set; } = null!;
        public string Content { get; set; } = null!;

        // FK – each post belongs to a single Blog
        public int BlogId { get; set; }
        public Blog? Blog { get; set; }            // navigation back to Blog
    }
}
```

> **Why `null!`?**  
> In C# 8+ we enable *nullable reference types* (`<nullable>` in the project file).  
> `= null!` tells the compiler “yes, I’ll populate this property before I use it”.

---

## 4.  DbContext – The “Session”

```csharp
// Data/ApplicationDbContext.cs
using EFCoreDemo.Models;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

namespace EFCoreDemo.Data
{
    public class ApplicationDbContext : DbContext
    {
        public DbSet<Blog> Blogs => Set<Blog>();
        public DbSet<Post> Posts => Set<Post>();

        // The DbContext receives options (like the connection string)
        public ApplicationDbContext(DbContextOptions<ApplicationDbContext> options)
            : base(options)
        { }

        // Optional: Configure entity relationships with Fluent API
        protected override void OnModelCreating(ModelBuilder modelBuilder)
        {
            // One‑to‑many: Blog → Posts
            modelBuilder.Entity<Post>()
                .HasOne(p => p.Blog)
                .WithMany(b => b.Posts)
                .HasForeignKey(p => p.BlogId)
                .OnDelete(DeleteBehavior.Cascade); // optional
        }
    }
}
```

> **Why a `DbContext`?**  
> It represents a *unit of work*: all changes tracked in the context are persisted together
> when you call `SaveChanges()`.

---

## 5.  Wiring Up – `Program.cs`

```csharp
// Program.cs
using EFCoreDemo.Data;
using EFCoreDemo.Models;
using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;

var builder = new HostBuilder()
    .ConfigureServices((context, services) =>
    {
        // SQLite database in the current directory (demo.db)
        services.AddDbContext<ApplicationDbContext>(options =>
            options.UseSqlite("Data Source=demo.db"));
    });

var host = builder.Build();
var scope = host.Services.CreateScope();
var db = scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<ApplicationDbContext>();

// 1️⃣ Create the database (or apply migrations)
db.Database.EnsureCreated();

// 2️⃣ Seed a blog + posts if none exist
if (!db.Blogs.Any())
{
    var blog = new Blog
    {
        Url = "https://example.com",
        Posts =
        {
            new Post { Title = "First Post", Content = "Hello, EF Core!" },
            new Post { Title = "Second Post", Content = "Another day, another blog." }
        }
    };

    db.Blogs.Add(blog);
    await db.SaveChangesAsync();
    Console.WriteLine("Seeded initial data.");
}

// 3️⃣ Query the data with eager loading (Include)
var blogs = await db.Blogs
    .Include(b => b.Posts)
    .ToListAsync();

foreach (var b in blogs)
{
    Console.WriteLine($"Blog: {b.Url} ({b.BlogId})");
    foreach (var p in b.Posts)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"  Post {p.PostId}: {p.Title}");
    }
}
```

### What the code does

| Step | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| `Database.EnsureCreated()` | Creates the SQLite file and tables on first run (quick & simple). For production, use migrations instead. |
| `if (!db.Blogs.Any()) { ... }` | Simple *seed* logic: add a blog with two posts. |
| `Include(b => b.Posts)` | **Eager loading** – tells EF Core to pull related posts in the same query. |
| `ToListAsync()` | Executes the query asynchronously and materializes the results into memory. |

---

## 6.  Optional: Using Migrations (recommended for real apps)

Instead of `EnsureCreated`, you normally generate migrations:

```bash
dotnet ef migrations add InitialCreate
dotnet ef database update
```

* `InitialCreate` creates a migration script that builds the tables.  
* `database update` applies that migration to the DB.  
* Subsequent changes to your model → new migrations → update database.

If you want to use migrations, replace `EnsureCreated()` with nothing (EF Core will
create the DB automatically when the first migration is applied).

---

## 7.  Quick Demo – Run It

```bash
dotnet run
```

Output:

```
Seeded initial data.
Blog: https://example.com (1)
  Post 1: First Post
  Post 2: Second Post
```

If you run again, it will skip seeding and just print the data.

---

## 8.  Common EF Core Concepts (Quick Reference)

| Concept | What it is | Typical usage |
|---------|------------|---------------|
| **Entity** | Plain class that maps to a table | `Blog`, `Post` |
| **DbSet\<T\>** | Collection that EF tracks | `DbSet<Blog> Blogs` |
| **DbContext** | Unit‑of‑work & repository | `ApplicationDbContext` |
| **LINQ** | Query language | `db.Blogs.Where(b => b.Url.Contains("example"))` |
| **Include** | Eager loading | `.Include(b => b.Posts)` |
| **Lazy loading** | Load navigation properties on first access (requires proxies) | `public virtual ICollection<Post> Posts { get; set; }` |
| **Migrations** | Schema evolution | `dotnet ef migrations add` |
| **Tracking** | EF monitors changes to entities | `SaveChanges()` |
| **Concurrency** | Detecting conflicting updates | `RowVersion` property + `ConcurrencyCheck` attribute |

---

## 9.  Wrap‑Up

* **EF Core** abstracts the database into a strongly‑typed object graph.  
* With **minimal code** you get:  
  * Table creation, relationships, CRUD operations, migrations.  
  * LINQ queries that compile‑time check your intent.  
  * Optional advanced features: lazy loading, explicit loading, raw SQL, etc.

The example above is fully runnable on any machine (no external DB needed).  
From here you can:

1. Switch the provider (`UseSqlServer`, `UseNpgsql`, …).  
2. Add more entities and relationships.  
3. Add ASP.NET Core MVC or Razor Pages to expose the data through an API or UI.  
4. Use dependency injection to inject `ApplicationDbContext` wherever you need it.

Happy coding with EF Core! 🚀
